{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786438856,"nonce":"8vtagkf6uk0fnlDTVEtjO4pcabE=","message":"Done — all four gates exist, are wired into BOTH .github/workflows/ci.yml and `just ci` in the same order, and **each has been demonstrated to fail against deliberately planted drift and pass after revert**, which is this issue's stated acceptance criterion.\n\n**G1** (Go DTO -\u003e OpenAPI) reflects every DTO/Request/Query struct and diffs json tags, required/optional, types, enums, nullability and validate bounds. Planted `planted_drift` on SilenceMatcherDTO -\u003e `the Go DTO serialises this key; the contract declares no such property`. Reverted, green. It had already found 13 real divergences, fixed separately.\n\n**G2** (running server -\u003e OpenAPI) is a NEW Go-native package, test/contract/server/. It assembles the real app.Container over a real migrated Postgres, mounts the real route tree, mints a real credential through the real Bootstrap path, and calls ALL 85 declared operations over HTTP, asserting per response that the status and Content-Type are ones the contract declares and that the BYTES validate against that media type's schema. 76 of 85 reach 2xx, and that count is a RATCHET so a collapsed seed cannot produce a vacuous green.\n\nDeviation from SPEC §L.8.1's named schemathesis, argued in test/contract/server/doc.go: it is Python where CI installs only Go and Node; oto's server cannot be started with a flag (it needs a migrated Postgres, a bootstrapped org, a credential and upstream fakes, and that bootstrap already exists in Go per ADR 0021); and no Python is needed for the assertion anyway, since OpenAPI 3.1 schemas ARE JSON Schema 2020-12 and test/contract/schema already compiles the contract from the same embedded bytes the server serves. What is lost is fuzzed request generation, replaced by the exhaustiveness ratchet plus negative probes.\n\n⭐ G2 found a real bug on its first run: `channels/service/tester.go` set `Permalink: res.Ref.ProviderKey`, but ProviderKey is opaque and provider-defined (the delivery UUID for webhook) while ChannelTestDTO.permalink is `format: uri` — so **every successful channel test answered 200 with a body its own contract rejects**. No provider can produce a permalink yet, so it is now null.\nPlanted drift for G2: made the version handler return `version: 1` and drop `commit` -\u003e `at '/data': missing property 'commit'`, `at '/data/version': got number, want string`. Reverted, green.\n\n**G4** (OpenAPI -\u003e valibot): more existed than expected — the generator and both npm scripts were already there. The checked-in validators.ts was 509 diff lines STALE; regenerated and wired `validators-check` into the justfile and CI. Planted drift: hand-edited maxLength(10000)-\u003e99999 in CommentRequestSchema -\u003e the diff failed the recipe. Reverted, exit 0.\n**G3** was demonstrated the same way for completeness, so the README's claim about all four is now true.\n\nHand-written valibot schemas remaining: exactly three files (Actions.tsx, SnoozeDialog.tsx, SourcesSection.tsx), all FORM-input schemas carrying human-readable messages, and every one pipes into the corresponding generated request schema as its final gate. **No hand-written schema describes an API response**, which is what §L.8.1 actually forbids — the migration is complete. Pre-existing gap left open: client.ts's getItem/getList still check the envelope structurally rather than parsing with the generated schema, which client.ts:260 documents honestly.\n\nOne design note worth keeping: G2's probe table is keyed by method + path template, never by operationId string literal — because coverage_test.go decides an operation is covered by grepping for its quoted id in any _test.go, so a G2 table listing all 85 ids would have satisfied that ratchet in one stroke and silently retired it.\n\nREADME.md's gate table and docs/ORCHESTRATION.md both updated from 'not built' to 'enforced'.","files":null}]}